                Digital's New PCI-to-PCI Bridge Chip
                 Extends Support for 64-Bit PCI Bus


  ...More I/O Slots for Systems, Adapter Cards, Embedded Devices...


MAYNARD, Mass., January 13, 1997 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced a new member of its family of second-generation 
PCI-to-PCI bridge chips.  The new 21154 chip is designed to extend 
the I/O capacity of high-performance desktop and server systems, 
adapter cards, and embedded devices based on the 64-bit PCI bus.

     The 21154 chip features 64-bit primary and secondary bus 
interfaces, which provide the high bandwidth manufacturers need to 
build high- performance PCI-based servers and workstations with more 
than the four I/O device slots supported by a single PCI bus.  The 
21154 complies fully with Revision 2.1 of the PCI Local Bus 
Specification, including support for delayed transactions, and 
implements an enhanced buffering architecture with deep buffers for 
increased throughput across the bridge.

     "Digital Semiconductor pioneered PCI-to-PCI bridge chip 
development and has set the standard for the majority of PCI bridge 
designs," said Matt Theall, Digital Semiconductor's marketing 
manager for PCI-to-PCI bridge products.  "The second-generation 2115x 
chip family is fully compliant with PCI Local Bus Specification 2.1. 
The 2115x family set new levels of PCI bus efficiency and performance, 
and the 21154 enhances I/O performance further with support for 64-bit 
primary and secondary PCI buses."

Extending the PCI Bus

     PCI-to-PCI bridge chips extend a system's I/O support 
capability by enabling motherboard designers to exceed the four-slot 
PCI bus limitation for PCI devices and option cards.  Each 
PCI-to-PCI bridge chip added to the motherboard creates a new PCI 
bus to support additional PCI slots or devices.  A PCI-to-PCI bridge 
also enables adapter card designers to overcome the single load 
limitation of the PCI Specification by providing an independent PCI 
bus on the adapter card, to which as many as nine devices can be 
added.

Improved Performance

     The 21154 chip implements the 2115x family's enhanced buffer 
architecture, which makes effective use of PCI Specification 2.1 
delayed transactions, thereby improving performance during high I/O 
traffic periods.  The architecture enables the buffering of all PCI 
transactions, including memory reads, I/O and configuration 
transactions.  The buffer architecture also supports simultaneous 
multiple transactions in both directions.  Deep buffers hold 
separate posted write, read, and delayed transaction queues.

     The 21154's on-chip logic provides programmable, two-level 
arbitration and secondary clocks for up to nine PCI bus master 
devices.  An ideal solution for high-bandwidth embedded designs, it 
contains a JTAG interface and four general-purpose I/O pins.  The 
chip is packaged in a 304-pin point ball grid array (PBGA) assembly.

Pricing, Availability

     The 21154 PCI-to-PCI bridge chip is priced at $43.10 in 
5,000-unit quantities.  Samples are currently available, and volume 
shipments are schedule to begin in March.  A 21154 Evaluation Board 
Kit, to enable motherboard and add-in card vendors to reach the 
market quickly with new designs, is also available now.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and 
multimedia, as well as low-power StrongARM microprocessors under 
